00:01:25.000 --> 00:02:14.000
And today I'm going to explore the psychology of feeling safe. I'm Dr. Marianne Trent, a qualified clinical psychologist. Welcome to Inner Work. In the main episode of psychology, actually, I was talking with Dr. Mia Hobbes and Dr. Paula Redmond about knitting. And one of the things that came up was the idea of safety, really, and how it seemed to give them those moments of deep exhale to be knitting and engaged in that in a way which just lights them up and helps them to relax. in a way that nothing else seems to do. And this idea of safety and the psychology of safety is one that I keep coming back to and tripping over lately.

4
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:59.000
I'd love to know, how would you know if you felt safe? When did you last feel safe? How would you know what that would look like? So let's take a little look at what safety is. I think firstly it might be helpful to look at it being an absence of something. of something. So that's no danger, no threat, no risk. And often when I'm with people, and we kind of do achieve that sense of safety, you do notice those deep exhale moments. And it's a really physiological shift when you have that. I hope you are regularly

5
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:31.000
experiencing moments of safety, if not, you know, period of and chunks of feeling safe and loved and contained. But what I know is that that is not the case for lots of people. Lots of people because of the way they've been raised or because of the relationships they found themselves in as a result of friendships or intimate partner relationships will be constantly kind of scanning for criticism, constantly on edge and expecting things to be, you know,

6
00:03:31.000 --> 00:04:34.000
problematic and chaotic soon. And so sometimes even when somebody's feeling safe, that can feel really threatening in itself because their whole kind of body, mind and soul, if you like, is constantly scanning for that threat and thinking it's all going to go to pot any minute now. So, you know, abort, abort, get safe, get safe. Because actually their nervous system has been raised and schooled on chaos and unpredictability. So when things feel consistent and calm and kind of like plodding along or like your needs are being met, that can feel threatening in itself. And as a result, there might be some kind of self-sabotage behaviour, so ending a relationship or, you know, doing something a little bit more chaotic or or finding fault in situations where there might not actually be any fault. and, you know, just kind of blowing up our lives a little bit.

7
00:04:34.000 --> 00:05:18.000
So I would invite you, if you're watching this, to reflect on what behaviours crop up for you when you feel safe. Are they always adaptive, which would be leaning in, taking those breaths, enjoying those moments, or do they sometimes look a little bit different? What happens when we're not feeling safe is that we will likely strive all the harder. We might overwork, we might outperform people or want to, we might mask our true feelings, we might push people away, we might people please, or we might just generally feel like we are burning out or that we're not truly being seen for who we are. None of that feels very gratifying.

